    "evidence_text": "Our tweet2vec encoder is publicly available.\n\nEstimating the statistical properties of these rare word types is naturally a difficult task.\n\nIn\n\nHeavy use of slang and\n\nUnsupervised approaches attempt to reconstruct the original text from its latent representation  ,  .\n\nWe propose a Bi-directional Gated Recurrent Unit\n\nRelated Work\tUsing neural networks to learn distributed representations of words dates back to  .\n\nto these problems.\n\nalso use a neural architecture, but compose text embeddings from a lookup table of words.\n\nWe use L2-regularization weighted by $\\lambda $ .\n\nWord Level Baseline\tSince our objective is to compare character-based and word-based approaches, we have also implemented a simple word-level encoder for tweets.\n\nThe input tweet is first split into tokens along white-spaces.\n\nthe rest are grouped together under the `UNK' token.\n\nWe also removed retweets and convert the text to lower-case.\n\nThere were 2829 unique characters\n\nThe models converge in about 20 epochs.\n\nCode for training both the models is publicly available on github.\n\nThese are listed in Table 3 .\n\nTable 4 compares the models as complexity of the task is increased.\n\nFinally, table 1 shows some predictions from the word level model and tweet2vec.",
